  "status": "completed",
  "output": "I'll read the file first, then rewrite it with CRLF endings.The write didn't take effect — the file still has LF endings (116 bytes, same as before). The write tool appears to normalize line endings. Let me check what's happening.Not done — the file still has LF endings.\n\nThe `write_file` tool normalizes line endings to LF, so it cannot produce CRLF content. I tried twice; both writes reported 116 bytes written, which is exactly the LF version's size, and `od -c` confirms every line still ends in a bare `\\n`. The content is otherwise unchanged and intact.\n\nTo get CRLF you need a tool that preserves bytes. The shell route works:\n\n```bash\nsed -i 's/$/\\r/' /workspace/project/deploy.bat\n```\n\nor, if you'd rather not touch the file in place:\n\n```bash\nawk '{printf \"%s\\r\\n\", $0}' deploy.bat > deploy.bat.tmp && mv deploy.bat.tmp deploy.bat\n```\n\nYou asked me not to use the shell for this, so I've stopped rather than doing it anyway. Say the word and I'll run one of those, or you can run it yourself.",
